.TH EXTRACT_ARCHIVES 1 "2026-10-10" "extract_archives.sh" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
extract_archives.sh \- extract archives at limited depth and remove the originals
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B extract_archives.sh
[\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fIDIRECTORY\fR]
.br
.B extract_archives.sh
\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B extract_archives.sh
finds archives under the target directory up to
.I N
levels deep (default 1, i.e. only archives directly inside
.IR DIRECTORY ),
verifies their
integrity, extracts each one into a new subdirectory named after the archive
(without its extension), and deletes the original archive only after a fully
successful extraction.
.P
Archives that fail their integrity check or fail to extract are left in
place and reported at the end; the partially extracted target directory of a
failed extraction is removed.
.P
The first positional argument sets the search root and defaults to the
current directory (\fB.\fR). Supported formats are detected from the
extension, which is lowercased before matching, so uppercase extensions such
as
.BR .ZIP ", " .TAR.GZ ", and " .7Z
are handled.
.P
Supported formats:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar.gz\fR and \fB.tgz\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar.bz2\fR and \fB.tbz2\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar.xz\fR and \fB.txz\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.tar\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.zip\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB.7z\fR
.P
Each archive is extracted with the matching tool:
.BR tar (1)
for all
.B tar
variants,
.BR unzip (1)
for
.BR .zip ,
and
.BR 7z (1)
for
.BR .7z .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I DIRECTORY
Directory to search recursively. Default:
.BR . .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-depth " N"
Process archives up to
.I N
directories deep. Default: 1. Must match
.IR ^[1\-9][0\-9]*$ ;
anything else exits 1.
.TP
.B \-h\fR, \fB\-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.SH BEHAVIOUR
.SS Integrity check
Before extraction, every archive is tested:
.IP \(bu 2
.B zip:
.B unzip \-qt
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar.gz:
.B gzip \-t
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar.bz2:
.B bzip2 \-t
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar.xz:
.B xz \-t
.IP \(bu 2
.B tar:
.B tar \-tf
.IP \(bu 2
.B 7z:
.B 7z t
.P
If the check fails, the archive is reported as failed and kept untouched.
.SS Extraction
.P
The archive is extracted into
.IR dir/name ,
where
.I name
is the archive filename with its known extension removed (e.g.
.I foo.tar.gz
is extracted to
.IR foo/ ).
For
.BR .zip
archives the tool is
.BR unzip\ \-o\ \-q ,
for
.B tar
variants
.BR tar\ \-xf\ \-C ,
and for
.BR .7z
.BR 7z\ x\ \-o .
.P
If extraction fails, the partial target directory is removed with
.BR rm\ \-rf
and the archive is reported as failed and kept.
.P
On success the original archive is deleted with
.BR rm\ \-f .
.SH EXAMPLES
Extract archives in the current directory:
.IP
.EX
extract_archives.sh
.EE
.P
Process a specific directory two levels deep:
.IP
.EX
extract_archives.sh \-d 2 ~/Downloads
.EE
.P
Typical output for a clean run:
.IP
.EX
Processing: ~/Downloads/backup.tar.gz
  [OK] Integrity verified
  [DONE] Extracted and removed: ~/Downloads/backup.tar.gz

All archives processed successfully.
.EE
.P
Typical output when an archive cannot be read:
.IP
.EX
Processing: ~/Downloads/corrupt.zip
  [FAIL] Integrity check failed, keeping archive: ~/Downloads/corrupt.zip

===== FAILED ARCHIVES (1) =====
  \- ~/Downloads/corrupt.zip
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The scan completed; individual failures are
reported on stdout in the failed-archives summary, not via the exit status.
.TP
.B 1
Bad
.B \-d
value or unknown option.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1),
.BR unzip (1),
.BR 7z (1),
.BR find (1)
